      "text": "1. **Determine the Least Common Multiple (LCM):**\n   - Darren works every 3 school days.\n   - Wanda works every 4 school days.\n   - Beatrice works every 6 school days.\n   - Chi works every 7 school days.\n   \n   We need to find the smallest number of school days (\\(t\\)) after which all of them will be working together again. This is equivalent to finding the least common multiple (LCM) of 3, 4, 6, and 7.\n\n2. **Identify Common Factors:**\n   - \\(3\\) and \\(4\\) have no common factors other than 1.\n   - \\(4\\) and \\(6\\) have a common factor of \\(2\\).\n   - \\(6\\) and \\(7\\) have no common factors other than 1.\n   - \\(7\\) and \\(3\\) have no common factors other than 1.\n   \n   - - \\(3 \\times 4 \\times 6 = \\)\n \n   - Using the greatest common divisor (GCD):\n\n3. **Calculate the LCM using the formula**:\n\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(a, b, c, \\text{and } d) = \\text{GCD}(\\text{LCM}(a, b), \\text{GCD}(\\text{LCM}(c, d), \\text{GCD}(\\text{LCM}(a, c), \\text{GCD}(b, d)))\\]\n\n4. **Reasoning**:\n   - Using prime factors:\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCD}(3, 4) = 1, \\text{LCM}(3, 4) = 12\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCD}(4, 6) = 2, \\text{LCM}(4, 6) = 12\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCD}(6, 7) = 1, \\text{LCM}(6, 7) = 42\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCD}(7, 3) = 1, \\text{LCM}(7, 3) = 21\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the LCM of 3, 4, 6, and 7 is 84.\n\nThus, the number of school days from today when they will all be together again in the math lab is \\(\\boxed{84}\\).",
