      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "1: To find the number of school days from today where they all day at the lab, find the least common multiple (L.C.M.) of Darren, Wanda, Beatrice, and Chi's working cycle (3, 4, 6, and 7 school days respectively).\n2: After computing the L.C.M. and adding the number of days they already work in the lab, you can find the number of school days from today when they all work again together.\n3: Answer: Their next time at the lab together will happen in 84 school days from today.\n\n\\boxed{84}\nTo determine when all four tutors will be together in the lab again, we need to calculate the least common multiple (LCM) of their working cycles. Each tutor works at fixed intervals: Darren every 3 days, Wanda every 4 days, Beatrice every 6 days, and Chi every 7 days. We need to find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) that is a multiple of 3, 4, 6, and 7.\n\nLet's compute the LCM of these numbers step-by-step.\n\n1. The prime factorization of the numbers is:\n   - \\( 3 \\) is already a prime number: \\( 3 \\)\n   - \\( 4 = 2^2 \\)\n   - \\( 6 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\)\n   - \\( 7 \\) is already a prime number: \\( 7 \\)\n\n2. Taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:\n   - The highest power of \\( 2 \\) is \\( 2^2 \\)\n   - The highest power of \\( 3 \\) is \\( 3 \\)\n   - The highest power of \\( 7 \\) is \\( 7 \\)\n\n3. The LCM is the product of these highest powers:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7 = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7 = 84\n   \\]\n\nSo, after 84 days, all four tutors will be back together in the lab again.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{84}\\).",
